WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.200
- [Narrator] Pardon me, professor.

00:00:02.200 --> 00:00:04.118
Maybe you can help me out here,

00:00:04.118 --> 00:00:07.479
is this word pronounced cache or cache?

00:00:07.479 --> 00:00:10.050
- Cache.
- Appreciate it, doc.

00:00:10.050 --> 00:00:12.740
You see, when Jacques
Bailly pronounces a word,

00:00:12.740 --> 00:00:14.557
he pronounces it right,

00:00:14.557 --> 00:00:16.950
he can pronounce this thing.

00:00:16.950 --> 00:00:17.848
- Smaradine.

00:00:17.848 --> 00:00:19.805
- [Narrator] He can pronounce this thing.

00:00:19.805 --> 00:00:21.031
- Scherenschnitte.

00:00:21.031 --> 00:00:22.597
- [Narrator] He even pronounces words,

00:00:22.597 --> 00:00:24.910
that don't make a lick of sense.

00:00:24.910 --> 00:00:25.776
- Albumen.

00:00:25.776 --> 00:00:29.070
- [Narrator] And when you're
that damn good with words,

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:31.670
sooner or later, someone's
gonna pay you to say 'em.

00:00:32.730 --> 00:00:33.743
- Hi, Dr. Bailly.

00:00:33.743 --> 00:00:36.260
- [Narrator] This is Jacques Bailly,

00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:39.330
the official pronouncer of
the National Spelling Bee.

00:00:39.330 --> 00:00:40.593
- Luxembourg.

00:00:42.810 --> 00:00:45.421
- [Boy] L-U-X-E.

00:00:45.421 --> 00:00:48.671
(lively Western music)

00:00:49.870 --> 00:00:50.703
- Gala.

00:00:51.800 --> 00:00:52.633
Sherbet.

00:00:53.790 --> 00:00:54.623
Gyro.

00:00:58.850 --> 00:00:59.833
G-I-F.

00:01:01.120 --> 00:01:02.390
- [Narrator] Before Jacques' days

00:01:02.390 --> 00:01:05.800
as the world's foremost
pronouncer of persnickety words,

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:07.150
he began a spelling career

00:01:07.150 --> 00:01:09.357
on the other side of the microphone.

00:01:09.357 --> 00:01:11.900
Jacques was a competitive speller

00:01:11.900 --> 00:01:14.352
and in 1980, he found
himself in the finals

00:01:14.352 --> 00:01:17.020
of the Scripps National Spelling Bee.

00:01:17.020 --> 00:01:19.980
His word, elucubrate,

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:21.330
so he said.

00:01:21.330 --> 00:01:24.199
- E-L-U-C-U-B-R-A-T-E

00:01:24.199 --> 00:01:26.490
and it means to burn the midnight oil,

00:01:26.490 --> 00:01:27.685
to study all night.

00:01:27.685 --> 00:01:30.170
- [Narrator] And with those 10 letters,

00:01:30.170 --> 00:01:31.980
Jacques became a champion.

00:01:31.980 --> 00:01:34.670
Twenty-three years later,
Jacques had elucubrated

00:01:34.670 --> 00:01:37.320
all the way to the big show.

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:38.930
- Propiophenone.

00:01:38.930 --> 00:01:40.420
Roquefort.

00:01:40.420 --> 00:01:41.823
Pyroclastic.

00:01:41.823 --> 00:01:43.896
- Can I please have the definition?

00:01:43.896 --> 00:01:46.370
- [Narrator] But sometimes even Jacque's

00:01:46.370 --> 00:01:49.153
meticulous pronunciations
don't tell the whole story.

00:01:49.153 --> 00:01:52.200
- English words are not
always spelled as they sound.

00:01:52.200 --> 00:01:54.870
One thing that makes English
words really hard to spell

00:01:54.870 --> 00:01:55.983
is the schwa,

00:01:57.970 --> 00:02:01.712
the schwa is that uh, like
in butter or unutterable.

00:02:01.712 --> 00:02:04.140
- [Narrator] The Y in syringe,

00:02:04.140 --> 00:02:06.000
the A in balloon,

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:08.010
the O in freedom,

00:02:08.010 --> 00:02:10.200
they all sound the same spoken out loud,

00:02:10.200 --> 00:02:12.800
a speller's nightmare.

00:02:12.800 --> 00:02:15.160
- The longer and weirder a word is,

00:02:15.160 --> 00:02:16.670
the easier it is to spell,

00:02:16.670 --> 00:02:19.618
take the state fish of
Hawaii, the humuhumunuku, ah,

00:02:19.618 --> 00:02:21.970
the humuhumunukunukuapua'a,

00:02:21.970 --> 00:02:24.600
that's an easy word to
spell, it's just long,

00:02:24.600 --> 00:02:26.621
it's a weird word and
weird things stand out

00:02:26.621 --> 00:02:28.302
and are easier to remember.

00:02:28.302 --> 00:02:30.550
- O-N-O-M-A-S-I-A,

00:02:30.550 --> 00:02:31.940
paronomasia.
- Correct.

00:02:31.940 --> 00:02:32.773
- Let's go!

00:02:32.773 --> 00:02:36.043
(audience applauding)

00:02:36.043 --> 00:02:37.295
- [Narrator] For a man with perhaps

00:02:37.295 --> 00:02:41.190
the most legitimate claim as
the authority on pronunciation,

00:02:41.190 --> 00:02:44.320
Jacques takes a rather agnostic approach

00:02:44.320 --> 00:02:47.270
to the right and wrong ways to say things.

00:02:47.270 --> 00:02:48.548
- A lot of people are kind of amazed,

00:02:48.548 --> 00:02:51.100
that I know how to
pronounce all these words,

00:02:51.100 --> 00:02:53.439
but I don't, I read them
from the dictionary.

00:02:53.439 --> 00:02:56.350
Well, how does the dictionary
know how to pronounce a word?

00:02:56.350 --> 00:02:59.600
Because they go out there and
listen to native speakers,

00:02:59.600 --> 00:03:00.433
if there are a whole bunch of people,

00:03:00.433 --> 00:03:01.980
who pronounce a word a certain way,

00:03:01.980 --> 00:03:04.188
that makes it a correct pronunciation.

00:03:04.188 --> 00:03:06.010
People have very strong opinions,

00:03:06.010 --> 00:03:08.490
that there's an incorrect
way to pronounce a word,

00:03:08.490 --> 00:03:10.278
but it's like saying
that dog barked wrong,

00:03:10.278 --> 00:03:13.529
that's not a bark, well,
the dog gets to decide.

00:03:13.529 --> 00:03:14.505
(barking dog)

00:03:14.505 --> 00:03:16.300
- [Narrator] Thanks Jacques,

00:03:16.300 --> 00:03:17.780
I reckon you got more wisdom,

00:03:17.780 --> 00:03:19.553
than all the books in the library.

00:03:25.133 --> 00:03:28.300
(light melodic music)

